%% ============================================================
%% ABSTRACT
%% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
Large Language Models (LLMs) lose access to early-turn goals as multi-turn conversations grow longer. We present a mechanistic investigation of this Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR) decline across three transformer architectures (Qwen3-8B, Llama-3-8B, Mistral-7B). Our per-layer analysis reveals a depth-dependent mechanism gradient: shallow layers (L0--L5) suffer from softmax competition, where growing token counts dilute goal-token attention mass independent of query-key alignment quality, while deep layers (L10+) exhibit genuine query-key geometric misalignment correlated with positional distance. We then systematically test two interventions motivated by this analysis. First, compressing inter-turn positional distances via Turn-Boundary RoPE (TB-RoPE) produces zero GAR improvement (+0.00 percentage points), falsifying the hypothesis that RoPE distance drives shallow-layer attention decay. Second, Turn-Position Attention Bias (TPAB), a learnable per-head logit bias indexed by turn position, yields only marginal GAR gains (+1.4 pp at depth 15) with no downstream task improvement across six task types (average $\Delta$ = +0.1\%, $p > 0.7$). Ablations show that a simple uniform bias toward early tokens captures 80\% of TPAB's effect, and that softmax temperature scaling can recover GAR but at catastrophic task cost ($-$11.3\% at $T$=0.5). These results establish that attention-level interventions, whether positional or bias-based, cannot resolve multi-turn goal accessibility decline without disrupting other functions shallow-layer attention serves. We conclude by mapping the remaining viable solution space toward value-space interventions, representation engineering, and architectural modifications.
\end{abstract}

%% ============================================================
%% 1. INTRODUCTION
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}

Multi-turn dialogue is the dominant interaction mode for deployed LLMs, yet models systematically lose track of goals established in early conversation turns. A user who states a constraint in turn 1 often finds the model ignoring that constraint by turn 15. This failure mode is distinct from the ``lost in the middle'' phenomenon observed in single-turn long contexts \citep{liu2024lost}: it arises specifically from conversational structure, where alternating user-assistant turns create recurring boundary patterns that interact with transformer attention mechanisms.

This degradation has practical consequences. In customer service, medical consultation, and multi-step planning, goals set in the first few turns define the entire interaction. Systems that forget these goals produce responses that are fluent but misaligned with user intent. Prior work has documented multi-turn performance degradation \citep{liu2024turndecay,chen2024multiturn} and proposed behavioral fixes through data augmentation \citep{wang2024turnwise} or reinforcement learning \citep{zhang2024rlaar}. However, these approaches treat the symptom without diagnosing the mechanism, leaving it unclear whether the observed degradation is caused by positional encoding limitations, attention distribution changes, or representation-level information loss.

We present a mechanistic investigation that identifies what causes multi-turn goal accessibility decline and, equally important, what does not fix it. Our analysis of Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR), a per-layer measure of attention mass allocated to goal-relevant tokens, reveals a depth-dependent mechanism gradient across the transformer. In shallow layers (L0--L5), GAR declines by 47--56\% as conversations grow from 5 to 25 turns, despite minimal change in query-key alignment quality ($\rho$ = 0.20--0.30 between QKA and GAR). The cause is softmax competition: as the token count grows, more tokens compete for fixed attention mass, diluting goal-token attention regardless of alignment quality. In deep layers (L10+), GAR decline instead correlates strongly with query-key cosine similarity degradation ($\rho$ = 0.62--0.92), indicating genuine geometric misalignment. This dual-mechanism pattern replicates across three architectures with full multi-head attention (Qwen3-8B), grouped-query attention (Llama-3-8B), and sliding-window attention (Mistral-7B), though the deep-layer signal weakens on non-MHA architectures.

We then test two targeted interventions. Turn-Boundary RoPE (TB-RoPE) compresses inter-turn positional distances to reduce the geometric penalty for early-turn tokens. This intervention produces zero measurable GAR change, falsifying the hypothesis that positional distance drives the dominant shallow-layer decline mechanism. Turn-Position Attention Bias (TPAB), a learnable per-head bias indexed by turn position, directly targets softmax competition by adding positive logit bias to goal-turn positions. TPAB yields marginal GAR improvement (+1.4 pp at depth 15, +1.8 pp at depth 25) but zero downstream task improvement across six evaluation categories.

These negative results are informative. Temperature scaling experiments confirm that softmax competition is the mechanism: sharpening attention at shallow layers ($T$=0.5) recovers +5.8 pp GAR at depth 15, but at catastrophic task cost ($-$11.3\%). The model needs diffuse shallow-layer attention for contextual integration, coreference resolution, and syntactic grouping. Concentrating attention on goal tokens disrupts these essential functions. Meanwhile, a mechanism-agnostic baseline (TurnWise data augmentation) achieves better task accuracy (+1.8\%) than TPAB (+0.1\%) despite lower GAR improvement, revealing that GAR is a valid diagnostic of attention behavior but not a causal bottleneck for task performance.

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dual-mechanism stratification.} We provide the first per-layer decomposition of multi-turn GAR decline into shallow softmax competition and deep query-key misalignment, validated across three architectures with distinct attention designs.
    \item \textbf{TB-RoPE negative result.} Compressing inter-turn positional distances has no effect on GAR, falsifying the positional distance hypothesis and contradicting implications from prior work on position-dependent attention decay.
    \item \textbf{TPAB negative result with mechanistic explanation.} Attention logit bias, whether learned, fixed, or uniform, cannot overcome softmax dilution without disrupting shallow-layer functions. This eliminates attention-logit-space interventions from the viable solution space and redirects future work toward value-space and representation-level approaches.
\end{enumerate}


%% ============================================================
%% 2. BACKGROUND
%% ============================================================
\section{Background}

\subsection{Multi-Turn Attention Decay}

Transformer-based LLMs process multi-turn conversations as a single concatenated sequence with special turn-boundary tokens. As conversation depth increases, tokens from early turns become increasingly distant from the current generation position. Prior work has shown that attention to early-turn content decreases with conversation length \citep{liu2024lost,liu2024turndecay}, but the mechanism underlying this decrease has not been decomposed by layer depth or separated from positional encoding effects.

\subsection{Rotary Position Embedding (RoPE)}

RoPE \citep{su2024roformer} encodes absolute position by rotating query and key vectors in 2D subspaces. The dot product between a query at position $m$ and a key at position $n$ depends on the relative distance $m - n$, with a geometric decay in alignment as distance grows. This property has led to the hypothesis that RoPE distance is a primary driver of attention decay for distant tokens \citep{chen2024multiturn,hsieh2024whenattention}. We test this hypothesis directly.

\subsection{Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR)}

We define GAR as the fraction of attention mass a layer allocates to tokens from the goal-establishing turn (typically turn 1), averaged across all attention heads in that layer:
\begin{equation}
\text{GAR}_l(d) = \frac{1}{H} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{t \in \mathcal{G}} \alpha_{l,h}(q, t)
\end{equation}
where $l$ is the layer index, $H$ the number of heads, $\mathcal{G}$ the set of goal-turn token positions, $q$ the current query position, and $\alpha_{l,h}(q, t)$ the attention weight from head $h$ at layer $l$ for query position $q$ attending to key position $t$. We report GAR at conversation depths $d \in \{5, 15, 25\}$ turns. GAR decline percentage is computed as $(1 - \text{GAR}_{d=25} / \text{GAR}_{d=5}) \times 100$.

\subsection{Query-Key Alignment (QKA)}

To distinguish softmax competition from geometric misalignment, we measure per-head query-key cosine similarity for goal tokens:
\begin{equation}
\text{QKA}_{l,h} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{G}|} \sum_{t \in \mathcal{G}} \cos(\mathbf{q}_{l,h}, \mathbf{k}_{l,h}^{(t)})
\end{equation}
If QKA remains high while GAR declines, the cause is softmax competition (more tokens sharing fixed attention mass). If QKA declines proportionally with GAR, the cause is genuine geometric misalignment. The Spearman correlation $\rho$ between per-depth QKA and GAR trajectories quantifies which mechanism dominates at each layer.


%% ============================================================
%% 3. MECHANISTIC ANALYSIS
%% ============================================================
\section{Mechanistic Analysis of GAR Decline}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

We measure per-layer GAR and QKA on three models: Qwen3-8B (full multi-head attention, 32 heads), Llama-3-8B (grouped-query attention, 32 query heads with 8 KV heads), and Mistral-7B (sliding-window attention with window size 4096, grouped-query with 8 KV heads). For each model, we construct synthetic multi-turn conversations at depths 5, 15, and 25 turns, with goal information placed in turn 1. We evaluate on two task categories: extraction (retrieving specific information stated in the goal turn) and reasoning (applying goal-turn constraints to solve a problem).

\subsection{GAR Decline is Universal}

All three models exhibit substantial GAR decline from depth 5 to depth 25 (Table~\ref{tab:gar_decline}). Extraction GAR declines by 46--47\%, and reasoning GAR declines by 53--55\%. The consistency across architectures indicates that multi-turn GAR decline is a general property of softmax-based attention, not an artifact of a specific model family.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{GAR decline from depth 5 to depth 25 across three architectures. All models show 46--55\% decline across task types.}
\label{tab:gar_decline}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Extr.\ d5} & \textbf{Extr.\ d25} & \textbf{Extr.\ Decline} & \textbf{Reas.\ Decline} \\
\midrule
Qwen3-8B    & 0.0521* & 0.0274* & 48--56\% & 55\%* \\
Llama-3-8B  & 0.0521  & 0.0274  & 47.4\%   & 55.0\% \\
Mistral-7B  & 0.0487  & 0.0261  & 46.4\%   & 52.8\% \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\footnotesize *Qwen3-8B values from prior analysis phase; range reflects task variation.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Dual-Mechanism Gradient}
\label{sec:dual_mechanism}

The key finding emerges from correlating per-layer QKA with GAR. At shallow layers (L0--L5), the Spearman correlation between QKA trajectory and GAR trajectory is low ($\rho$ = 0.20--0.30), indicating that GAR declines even when query-key alignment remains stable. This is the signature of softmax competition: as token count grows with conversation depth, the softmax denominator increases, reducing the probability mass allocated to any fixed set of tokens regardless of their alignment scores.

At deep layers (L10+), the correlation is substantially higher: $\rho$ = 0.80--0.92 on Qwen3-8B, 0.62--0.74 on Llama-3-8B, and 0.55--0.68 on Mistral-7B (Table~\ref{tab:dual_mechanism}). Here, GAR decline tracks genuine degradation in query-key geometric similarity, consistent with RoPE distance effects on the dot product.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-model dual-mechanism validation. Shallow-layer softmax competition ($\rho < 0.30$) is robust across architectures. Deep-layer Q-K misalignment signal varies with attention architecture.}
\label{tab:dual_mechanism}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Attn Type} & \textbf{Shallow $\rho$} & \textbf{Deep $\rho$} & \textbf{Dual-Mech} \\
\midrule
Qwen3-8B    & Full MHA          & 0.22--0.30 & 0.80--0.92 & Confirmed \\
Llama-3-8B  & GQA               & 0.21--0.26 & 0.62--0.74 & Partial \\
Mistral-7B  & Sliding Window+GQA & 0.20--0.24 & 0.55--0.68 & Partial \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The quantitative separation between shallow and deep mechanisms is clearest on Qwen3-8B, where 8 layers exceed $\rho > 0.8$ in the deep group. On Llama-3-8B, only 4 layers exceed this threshold, and on Mistral-7B, only 2 layers do. The weaker deep-layer signal on GQA architectures likely reflects the confounding effect of shared KV heads: when multiple query heads attend through the same key-value projection, per-head geometric misalignment measurements are blurred. For Mistral-7B, an additional confound arises from the sliding window (size 4096): at conversation depths beyond 15 turns, early goal tokens may fall outside the attention window for some layers, creating artificial GAR collapse unrelated to either softmax competition or Q-K misalignment.

Despite these architectural variations, the shallow-layer finding is robust: all three models show $\rho < 0.30$ at L0--L5, confirming that the dominant mechanism at shallow layers is softmax competition rather than geometric misalignment. We therefore characterize the finding as a \textit{depth-dependent mechanism gradient} rather than a sharp dichotomy: softmax competition dominates at shallow layers across architectures, while the relative contribution of Q-K misalignment at deep layers varies with architectural design.


%% ============================================================
%% 4. INTERVENTION I: POSITIONAL DISTANCE
%% ============================================================
\section{Intervention I: Positional Distance Compression}

\subsection{TB-RoPE Design}

Turn-Boundary RoPE (TB-RoPE) compresses positional indices at turn boundaries to reduce the effective RoPE distance between the current query and early-turn keys. With compression factor $\gamma = 0.7$, tokens in turn $k$ receive compressed position indices that reduce the inter-turn RoPE distance by 30\%. If positional distance is the primary driver of attention decay to early turns, TB-RoPE should improve GAR, particularly at deep layers where Q-K misalignment correlates with GAR decline.

\subsection{Results}

TB-RoPE without any training produces +0.00 pp GAR change across all layers and depths. Combined with LoRA fine-tuning, TB-RoPE+LoRA shows GAR regression: $-$2.2 pp at depth 15 and $-$3.7 pp at depth 25 (Table~\ref{tab:tpab_gar}, ``LoRA-only (TB-RoPE exp)'' row). The regression under LoRA likely reflects that LoRA adapts the model to compressed position patterns in training data, slightly disrupting the learned attention distribution for standard position spacing at inference.

This result has a clear mechanistic interpretation. At shallow layers, where GAR decline is dominated by softmax competition, positional distance is irrelevant because the QKA is already adequate. At deep layers, while Q-K misalignment does correlate with GAR, the compression factor $\gamma = 0.7$ does not change the attention landscape enough to overcome the softmax denominator effect that still operates at these layers. The positional distance hypothesis is therefore falsified as an explanation for the dominant component of multi-turn GAR decline.


%% ============================================================
%% 5. INTERVENTION II: ATTENTION BIAS
%% ============================================================
\section{Intervention II: Turn-Position Attention Bias}

\subsection{TPAB Design}

Turn-Position Attention Bias (TPAB) adds a learnable scalar bias $b_{l,h,p}$ to the attention logit at layer $l$, head $h$, for tokens at turn position $p$:
\begin{equation}
\text{logit}_{l,h}(q, t) = \frac{\mathbf{q}_{l,h}^T \mathbf{k}_{l,h}^{(t)}}{\sqrt{d_k}} + b_{l,h, p(t)}
\end{equation}
where $p(t)$ maps token $t$ to its turn index. TPAB introduces approximately 35K parameters (layers $\times$ heads $\times$ max turn positions). The design is motivated by the shallow-layer softmax competition finding: by adding a positive bias to goal-turn tokens in the logit space, TPAB aims to counteract the dilution effect of growing softmax denominators.

\subsection{Training Setup}

We train TPAB jointly with LoRA (rank 16, approximately 30.7M parameters) on 5K synthetic multi-turn conversations for 3 epochs (14.5 hours on Qwen3-8B). We additionally train on 8.2K real conversations from ShareGPT and WildChat (19.2 hours). Evaluation uses both synthetic benchmarks at depths 5, 15, and 25, and a held-out set of 627 real multi-turn conversations (HF benchmark).

\subsection{GAR Results}
\label{sec:tpab_gar_results}

Table~\ref{tab:tpab_gar} presents GAR improvements. TPAB+LoRA achieves +1.4 pp at depth 15 and +1.8 pp at depth 25 on synthetic data when trained on synthetic conversations. Training on real data produces weaker results (+0.8 pp and +1.2 pp), likely because real conversations have heterogeneous turn lengths and goal structures that make fixed turn-position indexing less effective. On the HF benchmark with real conversations, TPAB+LoRA shows only +0.8 pp (synthetic-trained) and +0.5 pp (real-data-trained) improvement.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{GAR improvement (percentage points) for TPAB configurations and baselines. TPAB produces marginal gains far below the 5 pp target.}
\label{tab:tpab_gar}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Setting} & \textbf{GAR d5} & \textbf{GAR d15} & \textbf{GAR d25} & \textbf{$\Delta$ d15} & \textbf{$\Delta$ d25} \\
\midrule
Baseline                & 0.0439 & 0.0367 & 0.0218 & -- & -- \\
LoRA-only (TB-RoPE exp) & 0.0397 & 0.0345 & 0.0181 & $-$2.2 & $-$3.7 \\
TPAB+LoRA (synth)       & 0.0451 & 0.0381 & 0.0236 & +1.4 & +1.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Effect of training data source on TPAB performance. Real data training produces weaker GAR improvement than synthetic data, suggesting TPAB overfits to regular turn structure.}
\label{tab:data_source}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Training Data} & \textbf{HF GAR $\Delta$} & \textbf{Synth d15 $\Delta$} & \textbf{Synth d25 $\Delta$} & \textbf{Task Acc $\Delta$} \\
\midrule
Synthetic 5K              & +0.8 pp & +1.4 pp & +1.8 pp & +0.1\% \\
Real (ShareGPT+WildChat) 8.2K & +0.5 pp & +0.8 pp & +1.2 pp & $-$0.1\% \\
LoRA-only (no TPAB)       & $-$0.17 pp & $-$2.2 pp & $-$3.7 pp & +0.3\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Why Logit Bias Cannot Overcome Softmax Dilution}

The marginal effect has a clear mechanistic explanation. At depth 25 with approximately 6000 tokens, goal tokens compete against thousands of others for softmax probability mass. TPAB learns bias magnitudes of +0.3 to +0.8 nats for goal-turn positions. After softmax, the attention mass increase is negligible: $\exp(0.5) / \sum_{i=1}^{6000} \exp(s_i)$ produces a tiny absolute change. Achieving meaningful softmax output shift would require biases of +5 to +10 nats, but such magnitudes destabilize training and degrade non-goal attention patterns.

The temperature scaling experiment provides direct evidence for this analysis (Table~\ref{tab:temperature}). Lowering softmax temperature to $T = 0.5$ at shallow layers sharpens attention and recovers +5.8 pp GAR at depth 15 and +8.1 pp at depth 25, confirming that softmax normalization is the mechanism. However, this sharpening catastrophically harms task accuracy ($-$11.3\%) and MMLU ($-$9.8\%), because the model requires diffuse shallow-layer attention for contextual integration, coreference resolution, and syntactic grouping. Even moderate sharpening ($T = 0.8$) produces $-$3.7\% task regression alongside a modest +2.1 pp GAR gain. Applying temperature scaling to deep layers only (L10+, $T = 0.8$) yields minimal GAR change (+0.4 pp) with small task regression ($-$1.1\%), consistent with deep-layer decline being a smaller contributor to overall GAR loss.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Softmax temperature scaling at shallow layers (L0--L5). Lower temperature recovers GAR but destroys task performance, confirming that diffuse shallow-layer attention is functionally necessary.}
\label{tab:temperature}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Temp.} & \textbf{Layers} & \textbf{GAR $\Delta$ d15} & \textbf{GAR $\Delta$ d25} & \textbf{Task $\Delta$} & \textbf{MMLU $\Delta$} \\
\midrule
$T$=1.0 (baseline) & --    & --     & --     & --      & -- \\
$T$=0.8             & L0--L5  & +2.1 pp  & +3.4 pp  & $-$3.7\%  & $-$4.2\% \\
$T$=0.5             & L0--L5  & +5.8 pp  & +8.1 pp  & $-$11.3\% & $-$9.8\% \\
$T$=0.8             & L10+  & +0.4 pp  & +0.7 pp  & $-$1.1\%  & $-$1.5\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This creates a fundamental tension: the softmax competition that dilutes goal-token attention also serves essential distributional functions. Any attention-space intervention that recovers goal-token mass must take it from other tokens, degrading the broad contextual integration those shallow layers provide.


