This result demonstrates that task performance is independent of attention-level GAR in the regime TPAB operates in (1--2 pp GAR change). The model's task performance relies on the full information flow pipeline: attention weights determine what information is read from key-value vectors, but the residual stream preserves goal-relevant information across layers regardless of individual attention patterns. Small attention redistribution at shallow layers does not meaningfully alter the information ultimately available in deeper representations.

\subsection{Comparison with Mechanism-Agnostic Baseline}

The TurnWise baseline (Table~\ref{tab:turnwise}) inverts the expected relationship. TurnWise, a mechanism-agnostic data augmentation approach trained on 50K multi-turn samples, produces minimal GAR improvement (+0.7 pp at d25) but substantially better task accuracy (+1.8\%) than TPAB (+0.1\%). TurnWise trains the model to handle multi-turn conversations through behavioral adaptation, learning to extract relevant information regardless of attention-level goal accessibility. The task improvement without GAR improvement confirms that task performance uses alternative information pathways beyond attention-level goal accessibility.

