\subsection{Single-Turn Capability Preservation}

TPAB+LoRA preserves single-turn capability: MMLU 5-shot accuracy regresses by only $-$0.3 pp (63.7\% $\to$ 63.4\%), well within the 2 pp acceptance threshold. TPAB-only shows $-$0.8 pp regression (63.7\% $\to$ 62.9\%), and LoRA-only shows $-$0.2 pp (63.7\% $\to$ 63.5\%). TPAB does not actively harm the model, but combined with flat task improvement, it functions as a practical no-op: it safely shifts attention patterns without translating those shifts into behavioral improvement.


%% ============================================================
%% 8. DISCUSSION
%% ============================================================
\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What Causes Multi-Turn GAR Decline}

Our analysis identifies two co-occurring mechanisms. The dominant mechanism at shallow layers is softmax competition: as conversations grow, the softmax denominator increases proportionally, reducing attention mass for any fixed token set. This effect is architecture-general and resistant to interventions that operate in the attention logit space. The secondary mechanism at deep layers is query-key geometric misalignment, where RoPE distance degrades cosine similarity between current queries and early-turn keys. The relative balance between these mechanisms varies with architecture, with the deep-layer signal being strongest under full multi-head attention and weaker under grouped-query and sliding-window variants.

\subsection{What Does Not Fix It}

\textbf{Positional distance compression} (TB-RoPE) is ineffective because the dominant shallow-layer mechanism is orthogonal to positional encoding geometry. Even at deep layers where Q-K misalignment correlates with GAR, the compression factor does not overcome the softmax denominator growth.

\textbf{Attention logit bias} (TPAB and variants) produces marginal GAR improvement (1--2 pp ceiling) because additive bias in the logit space cannot overcome the exponential normalization of softmax when thousands of tokens compete. The approximately 1--2 pp ceiling holds across learned, fixed, and uniform bias designs, suggesting it is a fundamental limit of this intervention class.

\textbf{Softmax temperature reduction} can recover GAR (up to +8.1 pp) but catastrophically harms task performance because shallow-layer attention diffusion serves essential language modeling functions. There is no temperature value that improves GAR meaningfully without task regression.

\subsection{What the Failures Tell Us}

The negative results narrow the solution space in a constructive way. Since attention-weight-space interventions are bounded by the softmax competition ceiling (approximately 1--2 pp) and cannot be pushed further without disrupting task performance, viable solutions must operate downstream of attention weights or outside the attention mechanism entirely.

Three categories of intervention remain theoretically viable:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Value-space interventions}: modifying value projections or output projections to amplify goal-relevant information before it enters the residual stream, circumventing the attention weight bottleneck.
    \item \textbf{Representation engineering}: directly editing the residual stream to maintain goal-relevant features, independent of per-layer attention patterns.
    \item \textbf{Architectural modifications}: introducing dedicated goal-maintenance pathways (e.g., auxiliary memory mechanisms or cross-attention to a goal register) that bypass the shared softmax attention budget.
\end{enumerate}

The TurnWise result offers a complementary insight: behavioral training can improve task performance without improving GAR, suggesting the model can learn to extract goal information through non-attention pathways (MLP memorization, residual stream propagation) when given appropriate training signal. This approach sidesteps the mechanism entirely rather than fixing it.

\subsection{Limitations}

Our analysis has several limitations. First, GAR is defined over attention weights, which are a proxy for information flow rather than a direct measure. Attention patterns and actual information propagation can diverge, particularly in deeper layers where the residual stream carries substantial information. Second, our cross-model validation is limited to three 7--8B parameter models. The dual-mechanism pattern may differ at larger scales where attention patterns exhibit different distributional properties. Third, the TPAB evaluation uses synthetic and semi-synthetic benchmarks. While we include a real-conversation evaluation set (HF benchmark, 627 samples), larger-scale evaluation on naturalistic multi-turn interactions would strengthen the task-level findings. Fourth, we do not evaluate the three viable intervention categories we identify, leaving their effectiveness as future work.


%% ============================================================
%% 9. RELATED WORK
%% ============================================================
\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Multi-turn dialogue degradation.}
Several studies have documented performance degradation in multi-turn LLM interactions. \citet{liu2024turndecay} measured task accuracy as a function of conversation depth and found systematic decline on extraction and reasoning tasks. \citet{chen2024multiturn} showed that instruction-following quality degrades with conversation length, particularly for constraints stated in early turns. These studies established the phenomenon but did not decompose the underlying attention-level mechanism by layer depth, which is the primary contribution of our analysis.

\paragraph{Position-dependent attention patterns.}
The ``lost in the middle'' finding \citep{liu2024lost} demonstrated that LLMs attend disproportionately to the beginning and end of long contexts, with reduced attention to middle positions. \citet{hsieh2024whenattention} analyzed attention decay patterns in multi-turn settings and implicated RoPE distance as a contributing factor. Our TB-RoPE experiment directly tests this hypothesis and finds no support for it as the primary mechanism at shallow layers, where the dominant decline occurs.

\paragraph{Attention bias and positional encoding modifications.}
ALiBi \citep{press2022alibi} introduced fixed linear attention bias as an alternative to sinusoidal position embeddings. Several works have explored learned attention biases for specific tasks \citep{chi2022kerple,li2023functional}. Our TPAB extends this line by indexing bias on turn structure rather than token distance. The negative result we report applies broadly: if learned turn-position bias produces marginal improvement, simpler bias designs (uniform, linear) perform comparably, indicating the intervention class rather than the specific design is the limiting factor.

\paragraph{Data augmentation for multi-turn robustness.}
TurnWise \citep{wang2024turnwise} proposed augmenting training data with diverse multi-turn conversation structures. RLAAR \citep{zhang2024rlaar} applied reinforcement learning to improve multi-turn adherence. These mechanism-agnostic approaches achieve task improvement through behavioral adaptation. Our comparison shows TurnWise outperforms TPAB on task accuracy despite lower GAR improvement, highlighting the disconnect between attention-level metrics and task performance.


%% ============================================================
%% 10. CONCLUSION
%% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}

We presented a mechanistic investigation of multi-turn goal accessibility decline in transformer-based LLMs. Our analysis revealed a depth-dependent mechanism gradient: softmax competition at shallow layers and query-key geometric misalignment at deep layers. We showed that two principled interventions targeting these mechanisms, positional distance compression and attention logit bias, fail to produce meaningful task improvement. Positional compression has zero effect because the dominant mechanism is orthogonal to positional encoding. Attention bias produces marginal GAR gains that do not translate to task improvement because the model's residual stream maintains goal information through alternative pathways.

These negative results are constructive. By establishing what does not work and why, we narrow the solution space for multi-turn goal accessibility preservation to three categories: value-space interventions, representation engineering, and architectural modifications that bypass the shared softmax attention budget. The temperature scaling experiment provides a clear mechanistic validation: softmax competition is the cause, but shallow-layer attention diffusion is a feature the model depends on, not merely a flaw to be corrected. Future work should explore interventions that preserve goal-relevant information in the value and representation spaces without disrupting the attention distribution patterns that serve broader language modeling functions.


